   974	    and `Checker` with `Rule`, `Subject`, `CheckResult`, `Refusal`, `check_correspond` and
   975	    `check_refused` (replacing `check_iff`), the rule-to-refusal gate and the rule/version lifecycle
   976	    checks (1.10): the diagnostics types of C9. Two levels are kept apart. Core admission
   977	    (`Core.Admitted`, no lexing, parsing or fragment) is bridged: `Core.Admitted (embed p) ↔` the old
   978	    `Checked p` for every old program, abort-free since the old kernel has no `abort`. Source-level
   979	    `Checked` (lexing, parsing, the fragment predicate, then admission) is what `check_correspond`
   980	    is about; it is not bridged.
   981	
   982	**Every phase-A step, like every later one,** gives each new rule a readable doc comment citing its
   983	decision row and at least one accepted and one refused example (`RM:331`). The per-rule gate landed
   984	with `Ident` (step 2: `scripts/spec/rules.sh`) and works on `RuleId`s directly: every declaration
   985	registered as a rule (a `RuleId` naming it in `Spec/Rules.lean`) has a doc comment and examples that
   986	cite that `RuleId`. Step 11 adds the typed check that every `Rule` constructor's `Rule.id` names a
   987	registered rule.
