docs/decisions.md:1370:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
docs/decisions.md:1715:- <a id="OPEN-126"></a>**OPEN-126** (OD126, Target assumptions): See [OD126](#od126).
   145	### 1.3 Types and linearity
   146	
   147	| Copy | Where | Notes |
   148	|---|---|---|
   149	| `IntTy`, `STy`, `Ty` | `Spec/Syntax.lean:31,64,70` | struct fields are scalars only |
   150	| `Brand.STy` (`alloc β`, `heap β`) | `Spec/Brand.lean:44` | the same name as `ConcreteSpec.STy`, with a different meaning |
   151	| `Authority.GrantTy` | `Spec/Authority.lean:96` | |
   152	| `Borrow.Place` flags `mutable`, `owner` | `Spec/Borrow.lean:32-36` | stand in for a type |
   153	| `Borrow.Source` | `Spec/Borrow.lean:133-136` | run-time data (array length, text bytes), which `RangeFacts` then also uses as a static fact (`:177`) |
   154	| linearity | `Ty.isCopy` `Spec/Syntax.lean:91`; `Val.isCopy` `Spec/Semantics.lean:72` (the same declaration lookup again); prose `Spec/Brand.lean:42` | three encodings. `Place.owner` (`Spec/Borrow.lean:35`) is root-versus-view ownership metadata, not linearity; it becomes the entry's `root` field (1.12) |
   155	
   156	**Unified:** one `Ty` in `Spec/Types.lean` with these constructors:
   157	
   158	- `scalar`;
   159	- `struct` and `enum`, the flat nominal data of Q9.2 and Q9.4, whose fields are any `Ty`;
   160	- `array T n` and `text`;
   161	- `alloc β` and `heap β T`, with brands as a separate erased kind (Q1.1);
   162	- `view m T`, second-class (Q3.1–Q3.3);
   163	- `grant g`;
   164	- `Result` and `Option` as ordinary enum declarations;
   165	- `secret T` on scalars (R-0596), from slice 7, within A2i.
   166	
   167	`Ty` is complete for every type the old fragments already use (alloc, heap, views, grants, text)
   168	from phase A, ahead of the slices that expose them in source: `Fragment.surface` gates the source,
   169	not the Core.
   170	
   171	There is one `Ty.isCopy`. One recursive `Ty.brands`, over struct and enum fields, gives the
   172	allocator dependencies of Q1.5 and Q1.6. `Val.isCopy` goes away: the run-time environment keeps
   173	each binding's declared type as instrumentation.
   174	
   175	### 1.4 Values and stores
   320	**Unified:** one total clock-indexed evaluator (Q9.9) with one fuel convention, and one relational
   321	`Eval`. Today `Proofs/Slice1.lean` proves `exec_deterministic`, but its adequacy is specific to
   322	straight-line code: `exec_complete` uses the fuel `ss.length + 1`, and `exec_total` holds because
   323	slice 1 has no loops or calls. Neither generalizes. They are replaced by:
   324	
   325	- `run_sound`: `run n p = o` with `o ≠ outOfFuel` gives `Eval p o`;
   326	- `run_complete`: `Eval p o` gives `∃ n, run n p = o` (existential fuel, no bound);
   327	- `run_mono`: more fuel never changes a non-`outOfFuel` outcome;
   328	- `eval_deterministic`;
   329	- totality (`Eval p o` for some `o`) only under a termination fact (3.1.10), never unconditionally.
   330	
   331	Each old evaluator is migrated with a correspondence lemma, proved in the commit that moves it and
   332	deleted with the old evaluator. For an oracle-free evaluator it relates the old run to the new run
   333	of the embedded program. For an oracle-driven one (`Brand`, `Cleanup`) it goes only in the
   334	realizable direction: every integrated run determines an oracle under which the old run agrees with
   335	it (section 6, phase C). The oracles `σ` and
   336	`ok` disappear: conditions and deferred calls actually run. Every fuel-relating lemma is stated once,
   337	at the evaluator.
   338	
   339	### 1.9 Instrumentation and erasure
   340	
   341	There are four instrumentation domains, with one erasure proved:
