-# Concrete Lean foundation proposal — draft 3
+# Concrete Lean foundation
 
-Status: proposal, not a completed freeze or approval of new rules. On 2026-11-07 the owner said, verbatim, **"ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?"** (owner answer supplied with this revision). This approves writing the shared foundation before freezing it; **pending recording in docs/decisions.md**. It does not approve the new questions in section 5.
+Status: approved by the owner 2026-11-08; the plan for PLAN steps 3+; the Lean spec stays the only home of rules.
+
+The owner approved writing the shared foundation before the freeze on 2026-11-07, **"ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?"**, and answered every question of section 5 with its recommendation on 2026-11-08, **"ok all recommended"**. Both answers are recorded in [decisions.md](LINK), which is the home of the decisions; ROADMAP R-0607 to R-0616 hold the work. The text below is the approved draft 3: where it says a recommendation, proposal or question, the recommendation is what the owner approved, and the revisions of section 4 are enacted as decisions.md records them. Questions 12 and 13 were removed as already decided. This document states no rule of its own: each contract becomes Lean definitions and theorems in [ConcreteSpec](LINK).
 
 The [A2i decision](LINK) freezes integrated scopes, "not an untested whole-language architecture". Here the frozen foundation means interfaces and laws implemented, proved and tested together, with representative interactions. It does not mean designing every future slice before slice 1. Later slices supply lawful instances; changes to decided rules return to the owner.
 
    99	
   100	### C7. Secret leakage and accounting
   101	
   102	**Freeze:** a separate leakage trace and public-observation relation; public/secret state equivalence; explicit release-site records; byte/resource accounting and their preservation/erasure laws. Leakage means information visible to the baseline timing observer, not ordinary I/O.
   103	
   104	**Rule:** leakage records branch decisions, exact addresses, allocation sizes, loop counts and operands of variable-time operations, with public lengths and contracted external effects. A release record carries ordered (typed site, released value), with reason/path metadata for facts. The release-policy interface lists allowed functions/sites or forbids release, checks reachable releases through calls, and permits only explicit declared values. Its security obligation is delimited release: for equal public inputs, matched external choices and equal ordered release site/value traces, prove equal public observations (NI), and separately equal leakage traces (CT). Policy authorization alone does not establish either theorem or allow other secret effects. These are distinct decided claims, not an optional variable-time Secret tier.
   105	
   106	Preserve wipe events as effects optimization cannot erase. Per allocator lineage report requested bytes, net change and peak logical bytes, plus allocator-defined footprint, each a sound bound with status or “not established”. Release/reset conserve accounting, and zero-size ownership adds no invented bytes. Bounds use polynomials with max over counts, lengths and depths; stack remains separate. This is the A2i instrumentation of R-0592, not later slice analysis.
   107	
   108	
   109	**Authority:** [information flow](../decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06), [memory/accounting](../decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06), Core Q9.10. **Later:** scalar secret rules/proofs at 7, arrays/wipes at 15 and heaps at 19. Binary constant time, hardware timing and actual wiping remain named backend premises until proved; speculative leakage is outside the decided observer model.
   110	
   111	### C8. Evaluator adequacy and termination
   112	
   113	**Freeze:** both the small-step relation and Q9.9's total fuel-indexed evaluator over instrumented hybrid Core state: `eval(target, layout, environmentChoices, fuel, coreProgram, state)`. This is the reference evaluator for qualification; it executes Core, not BIR or source text. Choices explicitly cover external responses, allocation/address choices, exposed-pointer alternatives and padding materialization. A choice input can be an oracle rather than a finite stream; each answer is checked against the operation contract. Oracle queries are pure deterministic functions of the Core execution history, request kind, logical site and occurrence number, independent of fuel budget; answers are deterministic functions of those queries and the fixed oracle. Materialization queries occur only at C4’s first raw exposure, not on safe borrowing or typed rewrites. Fixed inputs make evaluation deterministic. Native backends need not issue the same queries; P4 checks allowed Core observations instead. Prove evaluator soundness (a reported result has a matching finite execution), completeness (every finite execution is obtained with enough fuel), monotonicity (extra fuel preserves a completed result) and trace-prefix consistency. For nondeterministic effects these statements quantify over the same environment choices.
   114	
   115	Prove infinite-behavior adequacy too: compatible limits of fuel-indexed execution prefixes correspond to infinite maximal executions, and every such execution has those prefixes. Carry states and steps as well as event traces: silent divergence has infinitely many steps but only finitely many events; infinite interaction has infinitely many events. An endlessly blocked run retains its blocking classification. Independent finite witnesses do not establish a compatible infinite run. This follows Q9.9 and [A1, “Fuel and divergence”](../../legacy/docs/compiler/A1_BOUNDARIES.md).
   116	
   117	**Rule:** `outOfFuel` means only insufficient simulation budget. It proves neither divergence nor program failure. Divergence is an infinite execution; a blocked request is separately represented. Termination evidence is a theorem about all allowed runs under its named premises, not the evaluator returning once. Carry per-function termination permissions and evidence dependencies. Resource bounds count specified abstract operations; they are neither elapsed time nor evaluator implementation fuel.
   118	
   119	**Authority:** Core Q6.6–Q6.7, Q9.9, [bounded execution](../decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps), [step bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-step-bounds). **Later:** concrete variants, loop/call cost instances and user proofs. Q9.9 is retained, not replaced by small-step semantics.
   120	
   121	### C9. Identities, primitive contracts, evidence and ghost erasure
   122	
   123	**Freeze:** distinct typed identities for declarations/types, functions, binders/places, fields/variants, instances, sites, loans, brands, blocks, grants and obligations; capture-avoiding substitution and renaming laws. Names are presentation only. One primitive-contract interface supplies parameter/result types, authority, ownership/dependencies, pre/postconditions, memory footprint, initialization/validity, failures, events/leakage, resource effects and termination obligations. Every registered primitive supplies a complete contract; there is no catch-all unknown primitive.
   124	
   125	**Rule:** use independent typing, ownership, authority and execution judgments, with checker equivalence and elaborator correspondence. Keep safety evidence bound to typed identities, target, contracts and checker/spec versions. Recheck generic substitutions and Mono admission; compose checked summaries across calls/modules without reading bodies to guess effects. Struct-invariant rules remain those in the SPARK decision: private-field struct invariants hold at every exposure, with the spec definition written before freeze; the source feature waits for slice 17 (question 15). Contracts are erased logical assertions. Q9.11 and OD144 put ensures after all deferred cleanup, observing the result and only surviving parameter state. A consumed or scope-cleaned parameter is unavailable; body locals are never in function-contract scope. Entry values are read by requires, not implicitly saved for ensures. Q9.11’s authoritative row notes, [OD117](../decisions.md#od117) and [CON-3](../../legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md#CON-3) explicitly forbid `old` and snapshot constructs. Express relations to pre-state values of consumed parameters as Lean theorems over entry and normal-return states, without retaining a runtime owner or reviving cleaned storage. Safety admission needs closed evidence; assumptions cannot manufacture it.
   126	
   127	Prove erasure of ownership/brand/loan and accounting instrumentation and ghost computation: projected concrete executions and observations agree, and ghost state cannot alter runtime branches, authority or resources. Preserve diagnostic/evidence links even where code erases. Facts and diagnostics use one typed evidence graph and one versioned schema, with stable source sites; machine JSON views derive from it. A schema extension does not reinterpret old records.
   128	
   129	**Authority:** Core Q1.5, Q6.3, Q9.1–Q9.2, Q9.6–Q9.7, Q9.10–Q9.13; [OD81](../decisions.md#od81), [OD125](../decisions.md#od125), [pipeline](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [early evidence](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence). **Later:** primitive instances and syntax, module/invariant checking at 17. Q9.7's exhaustive inventory becomes exhaustive coverage of the selected registry, not a permanently closed universe.
   130	
   131	### C10. Frames and ABI
   132	
   133	**Freeze:** logical memory footprints and the frame law (an operation preserves disjoint memory outside its permitted footprint); call-frame ownership/restoration; ABI signatures relating typed arguments/results to registers, stack slots and preserved machine state. Include frame size/alignment, return convention, clobbers, stack-accounting handoff, extern validity/retention and launcher entry/exit relations.
   134	
   135	**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.
   136	
   137	**Authority:** Core Q3.12, Q8.5–Q8.6, [pipeline](../decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [contracts/P18](../decisions.md#decided-project-contracts), [stack bounds](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** ABI implementations, extern syntax at 25, stronger program frame proofs. Neither the frame interface nor packed access waits until then.
   138	
